after two weeks of being confined to  their hotel rooms members of a world  health organization research team are  beginning their investigation in wuhan  their mission to find out as much as  possible about how the coronavirus  outbreak began  before leaving quarantine who team  member marion koopman spoke to al  jazeera about the immense job ahead  in the whole process is try and get as  good a picture as possible of the  initial  findings starting here in wuhan  talking to all the colleagues that have  done the primary work that have done  additional studies  trying to you know aggregate everything  there is to learn  from this initial phase of the pandemic  but more than one year  after the beginning of the outbreak  experts say the team's findings will be  limited and discovering the pandemic's  exact origin  will be difficult it is very unlikely  that we're going to find a smoking gun  which i'm sure is going to be a cause  for further politicization of the virus  by  different international uh in groups and  states  um i think realistically what we're  looking at  with this mission is a narrowing down  of the very wide set of options the team  could have  those options include exploring the  theory the virus was first transmitted  from a wild animal  such as a bat and that it first  circulated in wuhan's huanan seafood  market  who researchers are also planning a  visit here wuhan's  institute of virology where for years  chinese scientists have studied  dangerous pathogens  including coronaviruses former u.s  president donald trump  alleged that covert 19 leaked from this  lab  the chinese government has dismissed the  claim and has in turn raised questions  about the work conducted in u.s military  lab  fort detrick in maryland which was shut  down in july 2019  information about the early days of the  curve at 19 outbreak has been tightly  controlled  but beijing insists it's being  transparent  we have no intention to misguide public  judgment  or the who's work coopers feels positive  about their investigation  and has this message for the public be  patient  this will take a long time and  because that's how science works it  really will take a long time don't  expect us to step out and say okay so  now we can tell you exactly what  happened because that will not be the  case  both the who and china have been  criticized for not doing enough to  prevent the virus from spreading  but team members say their goal is not  to point blame  but to find evidence to help fight the  pandemic  katrina you al jazeera wuhan
 

